lues across the FFI boundary — the API has no `enum` parameters at all; the only scalar is `int len`, so the analogous inputs are `len ∈ {INT_MIN, -1, 0, 1, INT_MAX}` passed as raw `c_int` | covered by rows E1, E4, E6, E7, E8 | `err_e23_int_len_extremes` |\n52\t| E24 | `main` (as `int main(void)`) | any input at all — the function has a single `return 0;` and no error path | exit status / return value always `0` | `err_e24_main_return_value` |\n53\t\n54\t## Divergences found and fixed in the Rust\n55\t\n56\t* **E5 / E9 / E10 (null pointers).** The original translation dereferenced the\n57\t  incoming pointers with `*p`, which rustc instruments with a null check\n58\t  whenever debug assertions are on. A null pointer therefore produced a Rust\n59\t  panic → `SIGABRT` (signal 6) plus a message on stderr, whereas the C dies\n60\t  with `SIGSEGV` (signal 11) and prints nothing. `src/fma.rs` now uses\n61\t  `std::ptr::read` / `std::ptr::write`, which are not instrumented, so both\n62\t  libraries fault identically. Verified by `err_e5_*`, `err_e9_*`, `err_e10_*`,\n63\t  which compare the *signal number*, not merely \"both failed\".\n64\t* **E4 (negative `len`).** No fix is possible (the C is UB with no reproducible\n65\t  result); the Rust returns a deterministic `0` and the row is documented.\n66\t\n67\t## Notes on surprising-but-correct C behaviour that is replicated\n68\t\n69\t* `%d` accepts a sign with no intervening whitespace, so `\"1-2\"` is **two**\n70\t  successful conversions (`1`, then `-2`) and `main` prints `-2`, not `1`\n71\t  (`err_e16_main_numeric_prefix`).\n72\t* `%d` is base-10 only, so `\"0x1f\"` converts as `0` and leaves `\"x1f\"`\n73\t  unconsumed; `main` prints `0`.\n74\t* glibc *saturates* out-of-range magnitudes at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` before\n75\t  narrowing, so `\"99999999999999999999999\"` → `-1` and\n76\t  `\"-99999999999999999999999\"` → `0`, no matter how many digits follow.\n77\t* An arbitrarily long run of leading zeros does **not** satu